24:<p class="kicker">Essay 7 · Lem ch. 7, "The Creation of Worlds" (2013 tr., pp. 237–293)</p>
30:<p>The chapter opens on a civilisation with a good engine and no rudder: "Civilization lacks knowledge that would allow it to choose a path knowingly from the many possible ones, instead of drifting in random tides of discoveries" (p. 237). Science wins its game with Nature every time and is dragged along by the winnings — "instead of developing a strategy, it ends up just practicing tactics" (p. 237). The remedy has to be an automated science, and he begins where the automation stops: "Many cyberneticists are currently working on the possibility of automating hypothesis formation" (p. 239), and the wall they hit is combinatorial. With five variables a machine doing a million operations a second gets through the values in two hours; "with six variables, the same process requires thirty thousand such machines, working at maximum speed over a period of several dozen years" (p. 239). His conclusion carries a qualification that decides how it should be graded: if these are random variables "at least for us, i.e., as long as we cannot see any link between them," then "no system whatsoever, no matter if artificial or natural, will be able to deal with more than several dozen variables, even if it was as large as the Metagalaxy" (p. 239).</p>
31:<p>So enumeration is out, and modelling has limits too — a model that keeps all the variables stops being a model. "A machine that would copy, with utmost exactitude, every material phenomenon would be a universal plagiarizer. Its full consideration of all the variables of phenomena would in a sense automatically cut it off from any creative activity" (p. 241). Between those walls he places his gardener: "A scientist wants an algorithm, whereas a technologist is more like a gardener who plants a tree, picks apples, and is not bothered about 'how the apple tree did it'. A scientist considers such a narrow, utilitarian, and pragmatic approach a sin against the Laws of Full Knowledge. It seems that those attitudes will change in the future" (p. 241).</p>
32:<p>The design follows. Since a fertilised egg computes an organism without writing anything down, "Could we not take up 'information farming,' cross-breed bits of information with one another, and initiate their 'growth' so that we eventually obtain a 'mature organism' in the form of scientific theory?" (pp. 251–252). The apparatus is specified in unusual detail for this book. The carriers are "large molecules of synthetic polymers" (p. 263) that grow by taking up "food" from their medium, and whose development is <em>isodynamic</em> with the phenomenon being studied — not a picture of it, a process running alongside it. They pass through containers he calls sieves; a sieve is "connected (e.g., by means of automatic manipulators, perceptrons, etc.) to a real phenomenon" (p. 264), encodes the phenomenon's actual state as molecular specks, and crashes the two populations together like antigens and antibodies. Carriers whose development predicted the real state survive; the rest are gone. Do it again on the survivors and you get "a theory of the examined class" (p. 265); do it on the theories and you climb toward "a theory of the class of classes" (p. 265). The law he installs in place of biological fitness is the crux of the whole thing: "that which expresses the environment most adequately survives" (p. 259).</p>
33:<p>Before the design he does something a forecaster rarely does: he states his rival's position fairly and declines it. Ashby's intelligence amplifier is built on a "generator of diversity" — any theorem whatever is already being transmitted, in Morse, by air molecules bumping into each other several hundred thousand times a second in every cubic centimetre, so "new theories count for nothing because they can be produced in great numbers by means of 'noise' processes… while the only things that really matter are elimination and selection" (p. 261). Lem's answer: "Our position on this differs from his. I have referred to Ashby here to demonstrate that it is possible to travel toward a similar goal… via different routes. Ashby thinks that one should start from the greatest diversity and then 'filter' it gradually. We, in turn, want to start from significant yet not enormous diversity—a kind of diversity that a self-organizing process (e.g., fertilized egg) represents—and make sure this process 'develops' into a scientific theory" (p. 261). He notes in passing that the generator already exists in a domestic form: "Mathematicians are thus a generator of diversity, whereas empiricists are the selector postulated by Ashby" (p. 261).</p>
34:<p>The chapter's second half turns to language, and it is worth reading against what this book's essay 4 records as Lem's big omission. Here he does treat language as a substrate: he sorts languages on a scale from <em>causal</em> — the language of heredity, where "word literally becomes flesh" (p. 273) — to <em>comprehending</em>, which is natural speech; he observes that natural-language utterances are "programs that are full of gaps" (p. 269) that the receiver must fill; and he forecasts a third language, built "in extracerebral material systems," which "almost by accident, will cross the threshold of 'understanding' or 'comprehensibility'" (p. 280) and by which "science will be producing predictions stripped of 'explanations' rather than explanations themselves" (p. 277). The last two sections are speculation and he says so: "The Engineering of Transcendence" builds an afterlife by machine and then argues itself out of it — "to prove belief is to destroy it" (p. 286), and the conclusion is social, not technical: "It is not a renaissance of transcendence that is needed but rather a renaissance of society" (p. 286). "Cosmogonic Engineering" designs worlds whose inhabitants must not be able to find the exit (pp. 289–290); this book scored the inhabitants' side of that in essay 6, and the constructor's side is still where Lem left it. The 1964 baseline: Ashby's <em>Design for a Brain</em>, perceptrons that could not yet read, machines doing a million operations a second, and no automated experiment anywhere. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964 --></p>
