58:<tr><td>A third language, built "in extracerebral material systems", crossing "the threshold of 'understanding'" and acting causally (pp. 277, 280)</td><td>Competence grown from written language, now acting through tools — the generator inside the search loops and the author of the workshop paper <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
63:<p>He knew the output had to be received by someone, and said so in a sentence the automated-science literature could use on its title page: "all information assumes the existence of an addressee. There is no 'information in general'" (p. 243). His whole farm is built backwards from that — the point is not to bury the reader deeper but to hand over what was extracted: in the future, he writes, scientists "will obtain its very essence and build their theories not from facts but from other theories" (p. 243). Compare the shape of 2026's automation, which is uniformly optimised for production: papers, candidate structures, hypotheses, all generated at rates no community of readers can absorb, with the reading left to the same overloaded reviewers who were already failing. He also insisted the sieve be wired to the phenomenon rather than to a judgement about it — the antigen–antibody image is doing real work — and every case in this essay where automated discovery held up is a case where somebody obeyed that rule. And he was right about the bargain the scientist would have to accept. The gardener who does not ask how the apple tree did it is now the normal working scientist in three or four fields, and Lem wrote the sentence in a tone of neither triumph nor alarm sixty years early: "It seems that those attitudes will change in the future" (p. 241).</p>
67:<p>He backed the wrong horse in an argument he set up himself, and he backed it for a reason worth naming. Ashby's proposal — generate wildly, then filter — struck him as wasteful and, in the air-molecule form Ashby gave it, silly. His alternative was to keep the diversity small and let structure develop, because that is what biology does. Biology was the wrong analogy: what makes an egg work is that it is not searching, and what makes discovery work in 2026 is that the search is cheap and the test is cheaper. The chemistry made it worse. Committing the design to molecular carriers meant committing to a substrate that has to be invented before anything can be tried, and he half-admits it — "we do not know how to build an 'information carrier' or where to look for the building material" (p. 266) — while a digital version of the same selection loop was already possible in 1964 and is what people actually built. And there is a smaller failure of imagination inside the successful part: his sieve selects for prediction against a phenomenon, which is right, but he never asks what happens when the phenomenon is expensive, slow or contested and the machine gets scored against a proxy instead. That is the failure mode the whole 2026 record turns on, and it is not in his chapter.</p>
80:<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "Information Farming" (pp. 239–266) is the design; pp. 260–261 is where he states Ashby's case and declines it, and pp. 263–265 is the apparatus. "Linguistic Engineering" (pp. 267–281) is the chapter's other half and the best answer in the book to the charge that Lem missed language. The last two sections are speculation he labels as such.</p>
81:<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> King et al. on Adam (<em>Science</em>, 2009); Boiko et al., "Autonomous chemical research with large language models" (<em>Nature</em>, 2023); Szymanski et al. on the A-Lab (<em>Nature</em>, 2023) and the Leeman et al. reanalysis (2024); Romera-Paredes et al. on FunSearch (<em>Nature</em>, 2023); Merchant et al. on GNoME (<em>Nature</em>, 2023); Lu et al., "Towards end-to-end automation of AI research" (<em>Nature</em>, 2026); Van Noorden and Singh Chawla on the 2023 retraction record (<em>Nature</em>, 2023).</p>
