+## Ranked findings
+
+### 1. Both MISS rows are invalid as written — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+
+**Location:** ledger rows “The carriers … MISS” and “His bet against Ashby … MISS”; lede, “the machine does not exist and will not” and “The filter won.”
+
+The polymer-carrier row does not meet §4’s definition of MISS: “did not happen **and there is no live path**.” Its only receipt is `funsearch-2023`, which proves that a digital competitor exists; it says nothing about the absence of a live molecular path. Lem also introduces synthetic polymers only “for example” (p.263), calls the farm “extremely difficult” rather than impossible, and on p.266 varies the possible substance and pseudo-organism form before conceding that the ideas cannot then be verified. Active molecular computation makes “will not” especially unsafe; even without adding new evidence, the registered receipt cannot pay that absence claim. Make the exact isodynamic molecular farm **OPEN**, unless a source can establish that its required mechanism has no live path. “Not built” alone is not MISS.
+
+The Ashby row misstates both sides of the comparison. On p.261 Lem explicitly says that his position differs, but the distinction is **greatest, random/noise diversity** versus **significant, limited diversity that develops**. He does not reject filtering as such: his own farm repeatedly generates variants and passes them through “sieves.” FunSearch is not Ashby’s air-molecule generator either. It begins from a program skeleton, samples a structured LLM, preserves a diverse pool, and iteratively evolves high-scoring survivors; GNoME uses trained graph networks and active-learning filtration. Those receipts prove generate/evaluate loops, not that maximum random diversity beat structured development. Remove this MISS or rewrite the row around the precise p.261 distinction and grade it **OPEN** on current evidence.
+
+These failures matter beyond two cells: the essay was pitched around two honest misses, but neither presently survives §4.
+
+### 2. The essay’s central claim about selection reverses the corpus — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+
+**Location:** lede, “the part neither of them thought was hard”; “the part Lem treated as machinery — the sieve — is the part that broke”; ledger row “Selection, not production, is where the value enters.”
+
+Ashby’s position on p.261 is that “the only things that really matter are elimination and selection.” Lem likewise spends pp.260–265 specifying an externally coupled selector and says on p.263 that setting up the farm is “extremely difficult.” The essay may fairly argue that neither anticipated **proxy validation** as the modern failure mode, but it cannot say that neither thought selection mattered or was hard. Rebuild the thesis narrowly: they foregrounded selection, but Lem’s proposed truth test assumed a reliable coupling to the phenomenon and did not analyze proxy failure.
+
+The ledger row compounds the problem. “Selection, not production” belongs to Ashby’s p.261 position, while its quoted support—“that which expresses the environment most adequately survives” (p.259)—is Lem’s fitness rule, not a claim that production is trivial. Re-anchor and reword the row before deciding its verdict.
+
+### 3. The 1964 baseline is partly false and mostly unreceipted — CONFIRMED; expands Agy 7
+
+**Location:** “perceptrons that could not yet read, machines doing a million operations a second, and no automated experiment anywhere. `baseline-1964`”
+
+The register row, filed under essay 4 rather than essay 7, receipts only the existence/dates of Beer, Ashby, Wiener, and Rosenblatt’s 1958 perceptron. It does not support the reading limitation, the operations-per-second number, or the global absence claim. The Mark I could classify simple visual patterns, including letters; “could not yet read” needs narrowing to Lem’s own p.153 claim that then-current perceptrons could not “read texts.” More seriously, “no automated experiment anywhere” is false: automatic titrators and clinical analyzers predated 1964, including the 1956 AutoAnalyzer and the 1959 Robot Chemist. Replace the sentence with a receipted distinction between automated measurement/analysis and autonomous hypothesis-generation, experimentation, and revision.
+
+### 4. The two object-level HITs are inflated, but Agy’s proposed alternatives need correction — CONFIRMED (Agy 1–2)
+
+**Location:** ledger rows “Automating hypothesis formation … p.239 — HIT” and “A ‘sieve’ … p.264 — HIT.”
+
+On p.239 Lem says that “many cyberneticists are currently working” on automatic hypothesis formation, and note 1 cites Samuel Amarel’s 1962 “An Approach to Automatic Theory Formation.” This is a report of an existing program, not Lem’s own forecast. Agy is right that the row cannot pay a predictive HIT from p.239, but wrong to suggest DISSOLVED or OPEN: neither verdict describes the problem. Re-anchor the row to Lem’s own p.243 forecast of a device that gathers facts, generalizes, checks the generalization on new facts, and presents the result to experts. That claim can plausibly take HIT against Adam/Coscientist/AI Scientist, stated at the demonstrated scope.
+
+The p.264 “sieve” row abstracts away too much to count as “substantially as described.” In context, the sieve encodes the phenomenon’s state into molecular specks and physically selects isodynamic, developing carriers. A-Lab automated synthesis and interpretation, but it did not implement that mechanism or an evolutionary survivor loop; its interpretation was also the disputed part. Either merge this into the molecular-farm row or anchor a narrower HIT to p.243’s gather–generalize–test–quality-control loop. Agy’s proposed RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER is possible only if the row is moved to the problem layer and names the live validation dilemma.
+
+### 5. The information-deluge verdict is not proved by its receipts — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+
+**Location:** “Automating production as the answer to the information deluge … RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER.”
+
+`retractions-2023` shows a record retraction year driven largely by compromised peer review and paper mills; it does not show that automated scientific production caused the deluge or failed as its remedy. `ai-scientist-2026` shows one of three generated submissions clearing a 70%-acceptance workshop after human selection of submissions; the paper discusses risks of noise and review overload, but a reported risk is not an event. Under AGENT.md’s reported-versus-true rule, this row is **OPEN** unless the essay adds evidence that automated production has worsened the information crisis or failed to tame it.
+
+### 6. The combinatorial-wall HIT is unverified, although Agy’s “reductio” reading is wrong — UNVERIFIABLE current verdict; Agy 6 REJECTED
+
+**Location:** “Combinatorial wall … HIT”; `scaling-laws-2020`.
+
+Pp.239–240 do not present a reductio that Lem later knocks down. Lem qualifies exhaustive enumeration to variables “as long as we cannot see any link between them,” then argues that hierarchy and selection of significant variables avoid ultimate modelling. Agy therefore cannot turn success through linkage into a MISS; Lem wrote that escape clause himself.
+
+The ledger still fails independently. `scaling-laws-2020` receipts smooth power-law improvement of language-model loss with parameters, data, and compute. It does not establish that millions of handled variables are linked, that nobody handles more than several dozen independent variables, or Lem’s universal ceiling. Add an appropriate complexity/statistics receipt and define “deal with,” or grade the argument OPEN. The current HIT is not verified by its register row.
+
+### 7. The language row violates the layer split and its receipts are semantically mismatched — CONFIRMED (Agy 5 and 8)
+
+**Location:** problem-level row “A third language … EARLY”; prose, “grown outside a brain, from written language, now acts on the world through tools … outputs we can use and cannot follow. `gpt3-2020`”
+
+This is an object-level forecast of a system/language, not a problem-level dilemma. Move it to Object level. EARLY is plausible, but the evidence must be split. `gpt3-2020` supports web-scale text training and few-shot behavior; it does not support tool use, search loops, causal action, or opacity. `ai-scientist-2026` supports an agentic tool-using pipeline, and `funsearch-2023` supports the search-loop generator, but the prose does not place those markers on this sentence and the ledger omits FunSearch’s marker. “Cannot follow” needs an interpretability receipt. Agy’s chronology language is imprecise—the essay does not claim that GPT-3 itself used tools—but its receipt-mismatch finding is correct.
+
+### 8. The 2026 register has additional semantic failures and unmarked claims — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+
+The remaining essay-7 rows broadly match their carefully qualified prose: `adam-2009`, `coscientist-2023`, `alab-2023`, `ai-scientist-2026`, `funsearch-2023`, `gnome-2023`, `alab-critique-2024`, and `retractions-2023`. Adam is “described as” first; A-Lab is “reported”; the critique is presented as a conclusion, not settled fact. The 2026 correction to the A-Lab paper should nevertheless be added to the register so the current state of the dispute is visible.
+
+The following do not match:
+
+- `gnome-duplicates-2025` supports duplicate findings and calls for correction/retraction, but not “a citation count in the thousands.” The cited report said about 1,000 citations. Cut the number or receipt it accurately.
+- Calling GNoME “industrial scale” is not in `gnome-2023`; it is a large research computation, not evidence of industrial deployment.
+- `baseline-1964`, `scaling-laws-2020`, and `gpt3-2020` are registered as essay-4 claims and reused here for materially broader essay-7 claims. The checker ignores the essay column and semantic drift.
+
+Material present-day claims with no adequate adjacent marker include: the farm “will not” exist/no live path; “nobody” handles more than a few dozen independent variables; systems handle millions only because they are linked; model outputs are usable but unfollowable; 2026 automation is “uniformly” optimized for production at rates no community can absorb; the gardener bargain is normal in “three or four fields”; search and testing are cheap; the digital loop was already possible in 1964; “nothing in 2026 climbs”; and cosmogonic engineering has no live path “in any form.” Receipt, narrow, or cut each.
+
+### 9. Three inline Lem quotations are not registered; direct-quote page numbers otherwise pass — CONFIRMED (missed by both reviewers)
+
+**Location:** “taking up ‘food’” (p.263), “a ‘sieve’” (p.264, prose and ledger), and Ashby’s “‘generator of diversity’” (pp.260–261).
+
+These quoted Lem terms do not have their own `checks/quotes.tsv` rows and are not contained in a longer registered excerpt that gates them. Add rows. Section titles in quotation marks can be treated as titles rather than quotations, but the checker should eventually gain an HTML-to-ledger reverse pass.
+
+All registered essay-7 excerpts were found on their stated page (allowing the intentional p.251–252 split), and I found no outright wrong printed-page number. One attribution should be made more exact: the air-molecule setup begins on p.260, while Ashby’s conclusion and Lem’s response are on p.261, so cite pp.260–261. The problem at p.261 is interpretation, not pagination.
+
+### 10. “Universal plagiarizer” is not a missing MISS — REJECTED (Agy 3)
+
+P.241 does not claim that learning from or recombining a large corpus prevents creativity. It says that an **exact material copy containing all variables** ceases to be a model and cannot perform the variable selection that theory and creativity require. Generative models are aggressively lossy statistical abstractions, not exact copies of every variable in the phenomena they model. Agy’s proposed MISS reverses Lem’s argument; if graded at all, the modern analogy tends toward HIT or remains an ungraded conceptual parallel.
+
+### 11. Agy’s generator-versus-selector remedy is unsupported, though the row needs repair — Agy 4 CONFIRMED narrowly
+
+Agy correctly detects that the row’s wording and evidence do not cleanly score one Lem problem-level claim. It does not establish its stronger assertion that the generator is “the hardest and most valuable part,” nor does that assertion make Lem’s p.259 fitness criterion a MISS. FunSearch itself says the target problems are hard to solve but easy to evaluate, while also treating the evaluator as essential. Fix the attribution described in finding 2; do not substitute Agy’s unsupported MISS.
+
+### 12. The ledger is rhetorically gerrymandered, but not for the reason Agy gives — Agy 9 CONFIRMED in result, REJECTED in rationale
+
+Specific component rows are required by CONTEXT §4, so atomizing a design is not inherently dishonest, and Agy cannot call the molecular design a “complete MISS” without proving no live path. The skeptical-reader problem is real for a different reason: two generic components receive HITs after their defining context is removed, while both advertised MISS rows fail the vocabulary test. After the corrections above, rebuild the ledger first and then make the prose match it.
+
+## Complete ledger disposition
+
+| Row | Current verdict | Audit disposition |
+|---|---:|---|
+| Automatic hypothesis formation (p.239) | HIT | **Re-anchor** to Lem’s own p.243 device; p.239 reports current work. HIT then plausible at demonstrated scope. |
+| Sieve coupled to phenomenon (p.264) | HIT | **Reject current HIT**; molecular/isodynamic context omitted and A-Lab is not substantially that device. Merge or reframe. |
+| Polymer/isodynamic carriers (pp.263–265) | MISS | **OPEN**, not MISS: “no live path” is unreceipted and the substrate is hedged. |
+| Bet against Ashby (p.261) | MISS | **Reject current MISS**: Lem rejects maximum/noise diversity, not filtering; receipts use structured, iterative generation. |
+| Combinatorial wall (p.239) | HIT | **UNVERIFIABLE** from `scaling-laws-2020`; source and terms need repair. |
+| Selection/fitness rule (p.259) | HIT | **Reframe before grading**: current title attributes Ashby’s selection-only claim to Lem’s different fitness criterion. |
+| Automation answers deluge | RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER | **OPEN** on current receipts; retractions do not show automated production failed as remedy. |
+| Third causal language (pp.277, 280) | EARLY | **Plausible EARLY**, but move to Object level and repair tool-use/opacity receipts. |
+
+## Minimum revision gate
+
+1. Remove or regrade both MISS rows; do not retain “will not” without evidence of no live path.
+2. Rewrite the p.261/Ashby thesis around random maximum diversity versus structured developmental diversity, acknowledging that both designs filter.
+3. Replace the false 1964 baseline and repair the three borrowed/mismatched claim IDs.
+4. Re-anchor or regrade the two HITs and make the deluge row OPEN unless causal evidence is added.
+5. Move the third-language row to Object level; add the missing quote rows and present-day claim markers.
+6. Re-run the ledger-first argument and ensure the essay retains one honest MISS or WRONG-ANSWER row under the exact §4 definitions.

